(T7, T9, B8). I have singles: W3, B5, B6, F1, J3. To get to Seven Pairs, I need to draw pairs for all these singles, which is unlikely soon. So Seven Pairs is a long shot.\n\n2. Aim for standard hand: Need three more melds and a pair. Possible pairs: T7, T9, B8 are candidates. Melds could be chows or pungs. With current singles, forming chows is hard because tiles are scattered across suits (W, T, B, honors). For example, W3 might form chow with W1-W2 or W4-W5, but W1 and W2 are low availability (3 each), W4 (4). T7 and T9 could form chows with T5-T6 or T8-T9, but T5 (4 left), T6 (3 left), T8 (2 left). B8 could form chow with B6-B7 or B9-B7, but B7 (0 left), B9 (0 left). So chow possibilities are limited due to discards.\n\n3. Consider discarding safe tiles to avoid giving points. Look at opponents' discards: Next player played J2, B4, W8, B9, B2, T1, W9, B7, T4. Across player played T8, Chi B4, played F3, B1, J1, B1, T2, B9, T8, B1. Previous player played B7, B7, B4, B2, B1, B5, T1, T2, T6, B9. Many B tiles have been discarded (B1, B2, B4, B5, B7, B9), so B5 and B6 might be relatively safe to discard. Also, T9 has not been discarded much (T9:4 left, only W9 discarded, not T9). F1 hasn't been discarded by others? F1:4 left, none discarded in history. J3:4 left, none discarded. W3:4 left, none discarded.\n\n4. Evaluate tile usefulness: \n- B5: I have one B5 in hand. It could pair with the B5 in the Chow? No, meld is separate. It's a single. Could form a chow with B3-B4 or B6-B7, but B3 (3 left), B4 (2 left), B6 (3 left), B7 (0 left). B3-B4-B5 chow possible if I get B3 and B4, but across player has a Chow [B3, B4, B5] already, reducing availability. B5 might be useful for All Fives pattern? Unlikely.\n- B6: Single, similar issues.\n- B8: I have a pair of B8. B8 could form a chow with B6-B7 or B9-B7, but B7 and B9 are out. So B8 pair might be kept as potential pair for hand.\n- T7: Pair, could form chow with T5-T6 or T8-T9. T5 (4 left), T6 (3 left), T8 (2 left), T9 (4 