e pung \u2014 so Dragon Pung contradicts All Chows. So must choose: either All Chows route (all melds chows) or include a pung like J2 pung.  \n\nWhich is easier? Currently I have one exposed Chow (dots), so All Chows means remaining 3 melds must be chows. Possible:  \n- Chow in hand: T7 T8 T9 (one chow) \u2014 uses T7, T8, T9.  \n- Chow: W4 W5 W6 (need W4) or W5 W6 W7 (need W7).  \n- Chow: leftover T7 with ??? Maybe another Chow from W3? W3 needs W1 W2 or W4 W5 (W3 W4 W5 needs W4). Actually W3 W4 W5 would need W4 and W4 is available (3 left).  \n\nSo if I aim for All Chows:  \nMelds:  \n1) Exposed: B5 B6 B7  \n2) T7 T8 T9  \n3) W3 W4 W5 (need two W4 and one W5 \u2014 I have W5, need W4, and have W3) \u2014 but I have W5 only 1, W3 only 1. Not enough duplicates.  \nAlternatively:  \nChow: W5 W6 W7 (need W7, I have W5, W6).  \nChow: W4 W5 W6 (need W4, I have W5, W6). But W6 is double, W5 single.  \n\nActually, my hand: W3, W5, W6, W6, T7, T7, T8, T9, J2, J2 (after discarding F1).  \nIf I target chows: T7 T8 T9 (chow), W5 W6 W7 (chow needing W7), W3 W4 W5 (chow needing W4 and another W5? I only have one W5) \u2014 conflicts W5 used twice.  \nMaybe: W4 W5 W6 (chow, needs W4), W3 W4 W5 (needs W4 and W5 again) \u2014 same problem.  \nSo All Chows looks difficult with current duplicates.  \n\nMaybe better to aim for 1 pung (Dragon Pung J2) + other chows:  \nThen melds:  \n- Exposed Chow (dots)  \n- Pung J2  \n- Chow T7 T8 T9  \n- Chow W4 W5 W6 (needs W4)  \nPair: leftover T7 or W6.  \nThat\u2019s possible, and gives: Dragon Pung (2 fan), All Simples (2 fan if no F1), maybe Mixed Double Chow? Not sure. Additional patterns: maybe Short Straight if two chows consecutive in same suit (but W chow and T chow not same suit). Could reach 8 fan with Closed Wait, Self Drawn, etc.  \n\nGiven this, **keeping J2 pair is valuable** for Dragon Pung possibility.  \n\n**Safety:**  \nWhat\u2019s dangerous? Others\u2019 melds: opponent across has B3 B4 B5 and W7 pung, so they might be going for All Simples or Half Flush? Not clear.  \nPrevious player has T3 pung.  \nDiscarding terminals/honors late could risk giving them big hands. F1 is East Wind \u2014 maybe someone needs for Big Four Winds? Unlikely.  \n\nGiven tile counts, F1 has 4 left, so safe to discard.  \n\n**Promising moves:**  \n1. **Discard F1** \u2014 cleans honors, keeps All Simples possibility, keeps J2 pair for pung or pair.  \n2. **Discard T9** \u2014 keeps F1 (breaks All Simples) but retains T7 T8 sequence potential.  \n3. **Discard W3** \u2014 keeps All Simples if I later discard F1.  \n\nBest is discard F1 now, then see next draw.  \n\nFinal choice: Play F1.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:**  \nDiscarding F1 removes the only honor, making All Simples possible, preserves J2 pair for Dragon Pung, and keeps sequence options in W and T suits. It\u2019s safe (many F1 left) and improves hand purity for potential fan accumulation.\n\n**Reason:**  \nKeeping F1 offers little value (not seat/prevalent wind, no sequence). Removing it opens All Simples (2 fan) and simplifies planning for other patterns like Dragon Pung + All Simples + Chow-based patterns to reach 8 fan.\n\n**Answer:** Play F1"}
